\DOC PURE_BOUNDER_TAC

\TYPE {PURE_BOUNDER_TAC : thm list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Proves a real-arithmetic inequality goal, or a conjunction of such
inequalities, outright by interval reasoning from a supplied list of bound
theorems.

\DESCRIBE
The single argument has type {thm list}: theorems supplying bounds on the
variables occurring in the goal. In the observed traces this is a large list of
upper/lower bound and defining-equation facts about the goal's subterms,
typically obtained from the current assumptions (sometimes with equations
oriented or rewritten relative to the assumption form). The proof draws on this
supplied list rather than on the goal's own assumption list. Given a goal whose
conclusion is an inequality ({<=} or {<}) between real-valued expressions, or a
conjunction of such inequalities, it closes the goal:
{
   A ?- t
   ========================  PURE_BOUNDER_TAC [th1; ...; thn]
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this one outcome: goals that are real
inequalities, each closed by the tactic given a list of bound theorems.

\ENDDOC
